16:role              = "face-proof — how a session proves ONE face at a gate without naming its vessel, under the PER-VESSEL shape: the face proof key per (PersonaGroup, Nexus) stays with the root-holding vessel and no private face key travels; each joined vessel mints its own face-scoped key, certified once at the join; a session signer chains beneath it. The gate reads face key → vessel's face-scoped key → session key, under one Nexus admit per face. Carries the key hierarchy, the gate exchange (a minimal Noise IK first flight in the WebSocket subprotocol token, provisional work only, a signed finish before any verdict), what the gate, a herm, a co-member and an observer each learn, the named leaks, causal deny-only withdrawal, the sibling channel's move to face scope, and what an offline root holder means"
30:''Two faces live from one vessel never link on the wire'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/the-session">>). A proof that names the vessel breaks that at the first gate two sessions both dial. So a session proves itself under a ''face-scoped proof subject'', and this carrier states how that subject's keys come into being, what a gate checks, and what each party along the way learns.
32:''Why this stands as its own carrier, beside the session contract.'' The session contract (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract">>) governs the inside of one vessel: its owner, its islands, its holder. This carrier governs the wire: a gate, a herm, a sibling and an observer read it, and none of them reads the holder's protocol. The two meet at one field, the session's proof subject, and each builds on its own order.
34:> ''Register.'' The operator ruled the browser's first proof (operator ruling, 2026-10-11; quote license: //"Approve your lean on the browser first-proof carrier."//). The per-vessel custody shape stands adopted by the house under the operator's standing grant; the operator read it and overruled nothing. The remainder reads as ''the designed shape''. #/owed names what stays unproven, and a builder MUST NOT harden an unproven part into code as law.
45:| ''Face proof key'', one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus) | the root-holding vessel mints it at random, independent of every other key, and authorizes its public half in private causal face state | the root-holding vessel; ''its private half never travels'' | a gate sees the public half as the admitted `nym` |
46:| ''A vessel's face-scoped key'', one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus, vessel) | the joined vessel mints it at random; the face key certifies it ONCE, at the join | that vessel's custody; its trusted daemon invokes it | a gate sees it and its certificate inside the encrypted exchange |
47:| Session signer, one per (session, Nexus) | the vessel's daemon mints it; the vessel's face-scoped key certifies it for a dial | a session-bound handle; it drops when the session closes or switches | a gate sees its public half and its certificate inside the encrypted exchange |
49:| Herm relay identity | the dialer mints a separate fresh signer for each herm dial | one dial's memory | the herm sees this key and a face-scoped channel tag |
50:| Gate bundle | the gate derives separate, domain-separated transport and signing keys per (tier, Nexus, route): a floor route's from the floor key, a serving route's from the serving key, a sovereign route's from the sealed custody root (#/the-gate-exchange) | nowhere: a bundle lives in memory from a derivation to a drop; a dialer pins it per (Nexus, route) | the dialer, and anyone handed the pin |
52:''The face's lineage re-issues the face proof key.'' The standing key and a successor minted at random both sign one hand-over; the record rides the Nexus's carriage board, the admit keeps naming the key the Nexus admitted, and a gate reads that key's line, stands a presenter under its head alone, and reads a line that forks as denied. A kapae on any key of the line holds the whole line. A gate that has not yet read the hand-over still stands the key it handed on, and no shared moment closes that gap; a compromise of the key therefore takes the face-key roll (#/causal-withdrawal), never a hand-over. A Handle card names its handle-KEL prefix, which no per-Nexus key enters, so a hand-over moves no card (house reading).
54:''A vessel's face-scoped key never derives from, and never equals, a vessel root.'' It stands independently random per (PersonaGroup, Nexus, vessel), so no key and no certificate field repeats across two faces of one vessel. The types forbid a vessel-root key and any cross-face field in a proof.
64:''`face key (the admitted nym)` → `the vessel's face-scoped key` → `session key`.'' The Nexus quorum admits the FACE key once per Nexus. A vessel's certificate stands as a delegate beneath that admitted face; it takes no Nexus admit of its own.
66:''The admit proves Nexus standing; the private face grant proves PersonaGroup capability. Neither substitutes for the other.'' A gate outside the PersonaGroup needs the Nexus admit alone. A gate that shares PersonaGroup planes also asks the ring to verify the private, root-authorized grant against that face's event history. No root edge rides beside the leaf (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/two-maps">>).
68:''A joined vessel receives no root bytes and no private face key.'' It mints its own key and receives a certificate. Theft of one vessel exposes that vessel's signer and no other's.
76:The dialer pins the gate bundle before it dials. Every signature uses its own domain bytes and a canonical field encoding.
79:# ''The gate answers the upgrade only after that flight decrypts.'' A flight that decrypts shows the dialer knows the gate's pinned key, which silence toward strangers protects. The gate then sends its 101 and selects the fixed protocol name alone. A wrong knock or a flight that fails to decrypt draws no answer.
82:# ''The presentation follows in the first encrypted frames.'' A face's presentation carries the face's public key, the vessel's face-scoped key with its certificate, the session key with its certificate, ONE presentation arm (an admit, a hosting grant, or an invite claim) and that arm's causal lineage, and the presenter's withdrawal frontier with any events the gate may lack (#/the-withdrawal-proof). An admit's `nym` MUST equal the face key. No ContactCard, no vessel key, no persona root, no root edge, no identifier shared across faces, and by default no Handle rides it.
84:''The admit's signed bytes carry the canon-document list and a validated full `GateBundlePin` for the hearth's sovereign sync route at its named Nexus. `readGateBundlePin` reads the object and its digest derives from it; the quorum's salted `carriageCommit` binds the opening inside the act image. A joinee reads the opening only through the admit at its held root (`verifySignedAdmitBundle`). Floor routes receive separate full pins through an authenticated relation or signed pin set; a floor announcement cannot authenticate its own first pin. `device-admit.ts` mints the internal `device-admit/v1` edge, and the fleet answer carries the pin and canon list.
86:# ''The gate checks, still provisionally.'' It checks the pinned route and audience, the chain, the presentation's signatures, the quorum's signatures, the causal parents, the deny events and withdrawals it has learned (#/causal-withdrawal), and the roll anchors. Where the asked planes need it, the ring checks the PersonaGroup grant. Missing required history, a known fork or an incomplete board brings abstention. The gate reveals no differentiated refusal.
89:# ''Both ends close the dial'' and discard its transport and herm-dial keys. The daemon drops the session signer when its session ends or switches.
95:* ''The knock derives from the bundle's digest'', and the handshake's prologue holds that digest. So a gate's knock, its pin, its verdict and its posture all read per Nexus exactly, and a first flight decrypts only at the gate whose pin its dialer held.
100:* ''A gate learns and keeps withdrawal events only under a face key whose admit it just read held'', and a named state goes to no other presenter. A face no quorum admitted makes a gate hold nothing.
101:* ''An OPEN gate answers a stranger.'' A sound chain under a face no quorum admitted, presenting no arm, sorts as a stranger where this Nexus reads OPEN: the gate answers `verified`, seats it at the public shelf and keeps nothing of that face's history. Where the Nexus reads PRIVATE the same presenter draws silence. The four states that refuse (`unconfirmed`, `withdrawn`, `incomplete`, `forked`) go to a presenter of an admitted face alone.
105:* ''Whether a hosting grant or an invite claim holds stays the sorter's own reading.'' The gate binds the arm to the face key and the Nexus, and attaches a walker only where the sorter read one.
110:!!! The tiers of gate ~ the floor answers locked, the sovereign gate answers raised, the serving gate answers an ember's raise
114:''A route names its tier, and a tier names its root.'' A ''floor route'' answers at a locked vessel: the public shelf, the oracle's read face, the floor's announcement, and a herm's blind relay of bytes it cannot open. Its bundle derives from the floor key. A ''sovereign route'' seats a face's session, syncs a private document or serves a lane whose bytes this vessel can open. Its bundle derives from the sealed custody root, and it exists only while the vessel stands unlocked. A route's typed name carries its tier, the pin states it and the pin's digest binds it: no route asks the other tier's root, a root asked for the wrong tier refuses by name, and a dialer that pinned one tier believes no gate of the other.
116:''Each derivation takes the tier, the Nexus and the route under its own name.'' No pin, key, digest or knock of one tier, one Nexus or one route links to another for an outsider: one vessel's floor pin and its sovereign pin for one Nexus share nothing.
118:''A dialer names the tier it means to reach'', and a pin of another tier dials nothing: a floor gate's `verified` seats the floor alone, so no caller mistakes it for a sovereign seat.
120:''What each tier costs.'' A thief who holds the disk and no passphrase holds the floor key: that thief can stand the floor gates, and can speak as the PLACE wherever the place's own key speaks (its carrier seal, a place arm), which reaches the public floor and the blind carriage of bytes it cannot open. That thief can never stand a sovereign gate, seat a face or open a sealed byte, and stands a serving gate only where the vessel's embers answer its copy (the serving tier, below).
122:''A floor gate seats no fleet lane.'' It asks no private grant and sorts nobody same-operator, it reads no withdrawal memory and answers no refusing state, and it marks every seat it opens as the floor's, so that seat reaches the public shelf and what the floor serves, and nothing this vessel can open. A presenter that asks more dials the sovereign route, and finds no gate there while the vessel rests locked. A floor route a design adds keeps this law: the ember's route (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-raise">>) seats a dialer by the transport key a standing ember pin names, and no device of the person's fleet by its fleet key.
126:> ''House ruling under the operator's delegation'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>); it reopens on proof that a gate pin decides no authority anywhere.
128:''A serving route answers at a vessel its embers raised to SERVING'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>): the serving gate for presenters already standing, and blind carriage for them. Its bundle derives from the serving key under its own registered domain, and it exists only while the vessel stands at SERVING or unlocked. The floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched: no serving route asks the floor key or the custody root, and a dialer that pinned the sovereign tier believes no serving gate. A serving gate signs its own handshake and no act, and its seat reaches what the serving tier holds and nothing beyond it.
130:''A pin moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step'', at every tier: a roll the vessel signs, a key rotation its key history verifies, or a removal a counted act carries. No quiet period ages a pin out, and no clock retires one.
136:''A herm or a carrier holds no face'', so it holds no face key, no certificate and no session certificate. It still dials gates. The presentation therefore takes a fourth arm, the ''place arm'', and that arm stands OUTSIDE the face proof: no chain rides it and no withdrawal history answers it.
139:* ''It presents the contract that names that key and this gate's Nexus'': the quorum's `carry` act with the place's own carrier seal. The gate reads it through the same held-root door an admit passes, against the board's counted `uncarry` acts and the antigen, and consults the edge shadow ring for the carriage edge.
142:* ''A standing names the presenter's own key.'' Where the sorter names a standing, for a place or for a face, it stands under the key that proved in this dial and at this gate's Nexus, or the dial draws silence.
145:* ''A place with no contract'' that dials a floor route draws what a proven stranger draws there.
149:▢ ''OWED'': an operator's contract over their own herm (one signature, one place, one Nexus) names bytes no code holds yet; the arm reads the quorum's `carry` act until it does.
163:''What the measurement read'' (one run, a house reading of its instruments): Chromium 156, Firefox 157 and WebKit 27.2 under Playwright 1.64, over plain sockets on loopback, direct and behind `nginx:1.30-alpine` under the household terminator's own settings.
166:| the header as sent | the list in the order given, joined by `, ` |
178:<<~ ahu #/who-learns-what>>
180:!! What each party learns
182:|! Reader |! Learns |! Never learns |
183:| The Nexus gate | the stable face key AT THAT NEXUS, so it correlates that face's sessions there (ruled acceptable: two sessions of one face may correlate); the presenting vessel's face-scoped key; the session key; the presentation and its lineage; route and audience | a vessel root key, a persona root, a root edge, or the number of vessels a face holds |
184:| A herm | a per-dial relay key, a face-scoped channel tag, routing, locators, timing, sizes and destination; a stable tag lets it correlate ONE face's carriage | the sibling proof, document content, any certificate in the chain, or a tag two faces share |
185:| An authorized co-member of the PersonaGroup | the private authority history, the planes its grant allows, and the certificate history, so it relates a vessel's face-scoped key to that vessel's membership | a private face key: none travels, so no co-member acts as the whole face |
190:<<~ ahu #/named-leaks>>
192:!! The leaks, named
194:* ''The gate can count the vessel keys it has OBSERVED under one face.'' A second vessel presenting under the same `nym` tells that gate at least two vessels wore the face there. It learns no total unless every vessel presents. A gate MUST NOT keep an inventory of a face's current vessels: that inventory would stand as a roster.
195:* ''A gate that has not learned a withdrawal may still accept.'' No clock repairs that (#/causal-withdrawal), and no proof without a clock, a roster or an online responder bounds it.
196:* ''A withdrawal history's length tells each gate the face dials how many keys that face has withdrawn there.'' A gate that never met a withdrawn vessel learns the count and nothing of the key.
200:* ''The knock tests offline'' for an observer holding candidate gate keys: accepted as named, since any pre-filter keyed on public pin material tests the same way (the reading and its measurements stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w28-gate-lane">> #/the-knock-reading).
207:!! Causal withdrawal ~ deny-only, learned, never timed
210:* ''Removing one vessel withdraws ONE certificate.'' The face key signs a causal withdrawal into a ''deny-only withdrawal history'' (#/the-withdrawal-proof). A gate checks a presented chain against the withdrawals it has learned. It never consults a positive list of current vessels, and it never asks an online service whether a key still belongs. The PersonaGroup's own membership withdrawal, its rekey and the re-sealing of standing content run beside it. No face-key roll and no fresh Nexus admit follows from one vessel's removal.
211:* ''A gate enforces what it has learned.'' A gate that has received a withdrawal refuses the withdrawn chain. A gate that knows of missing or competing required history abstains. A partitioned gate that has learned nothing may accept against its last verified view. No wall clock and no quiet period supplies the knowledge it lacks (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/no-global-now">>).
212:* ''A wiped device stands as a new vessel.'' It mints new roots, joins again and takes a new certificate. No earlier key reaches it, and no other vessel's key rolls.
213:* ''A face-key roll'' answers a compromise of the root-held face key itself: a private causal authorization, then the affected Nexus boards' revoke and admit. No clock lapses either relation.
221:> ''Register.'' The house wrote this shape under the operator's standing grant, after one round against the field's prior art: KERI's receipts and watchers, SPKI's online tests, certificate transparency and its gossip, Matrix device lists, Signal's Sesame, MLS removal and Keyhive's revocation. The whole section reads as a house reading the operator may overturn. The round found no construction that bounds an uninformed gate without a clock, a roster or an online responder, and this proof claims none.
223:''The face key alone authors the history.'' One history stands per face key, so one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus). Each event carries the face key, the Nexus, its position, the head of the event before it, and a digest of the withdrawn vessel's face-scoped key. The face key signs it under the withdrawal's own domain. The first event names a genesis head derived from the face key and the Nexus. No quorum signs and no board carries the history: the Nexus admitted the face key once, and a vessel's certificate stands as a delegate beneath it.
225:''A denial names a key by digest.'' The digest binds the face key and the Nexus, so a gate that never met the vessel learns nothing of its key, and no two histories share a value. The denial covers every certificate the face key ever signs for that key. Every denial the face key signed counts wherever a reader verified it, whether or not the reader holds the events before it. ''A withdrawn key never returns'': a vessel that joins again mints a fresh key and takes a fresh certificate (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/re-admission">>).
234:| `verified` | every signature and binding holds, the presenter's frontier meets the gate's own, and no learned denial names the key | it attaches the session, and keeps any events the presenter carried | it acts |
235:| `unconfirmed` | the chain holds and no learned denial names the key, and the gate holds a later frontier than the presenter showed | it attaches nothing and hands over the events the presenter lacks | it acts on nothing shared, folds the events, and presents again under the later frontier |
236:| `withdrawn` | a learned denial names the key | it refuses and hands over the denial | it stands withdrawn, says so, and leaves every channel of that face |
237:| `incomplete` | the presenter names a frontier the gate cannot join to its own: a later one with events missing, or another head at a position the gate holds | it abstains and asks for the history from its genesis | it carries the whole history |
243:''A gate's learned state only accretes.'' Learning reads as a union of verified events, whatever order they arrive in. An older frontier never replaces a later one, a denial once counted keeps counting through a fork and after it, and a fork once proven stays proven. A presentation that carries one event the gate cannot verify teaches the gate nothing.
255:''A gate that forgets a denial admits the withdrawn vessel again'', so what a sovereign gate learned outlives a restart. It rests in the gate's own vessel store, sealed under the vessel key behind the vessel's one store door, and on no board another operator reads.
258:* ''One row per face key'', named by the digest that binds that face key to this Nexus, and at most 1024 events in it; a 1025th refuses. The gate reads one row for one presenter and never walks the rows. One writer moves a row at a time, and one vessel holds its store, so no two keeps lose each other's event.
260:* ''It passes the registry test'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter#/the-test">>): each event stands authored by its subject's own hand, the gate reads it as of its own last learning, and the store offers no enumeration.
261:* ''Who may read it'': the unlocked vessel's own sovereign gate, and through that gate a presenter whose chain verified under that face key. A locked vessel reads none of it, and a floor gate asks none of it.
262:* ''A fresh vessel knows nothing'', and accepts as a gate that learned nothing accepts (#/named-leaks), until the first honest vessel of a face dials it.
263:* ''A store rolled back forgets.'' Every event in an older copy of a row still verifies, so a hand that restores one makes the gate forget what it learned since. No fence here stops that hand; the next honest vessel of the face teaches the gate again.
268:| a gate that learned nothing newer, facing the withdrawn vessel | that gate accepts the withdrawn vessel under the admitted face | the first honest vessel of that face to dial the gate; nothing else in this proof |
269:| a withdrawn vessel that shows an older frontier | nothing at a gate that holds the denial: the denial names its key whatever frontier it claims. At a gate that never learned, the row above | the same |
270:| a relay or a herm that withholds | nothing: the history rides inside the encrypted exchange between a presenter and the gate | |
279:''Two acts remove a vessel, and each answers to its own readers.'' The dyad marker sets a device aside inside its fleet: it rides the fleet's edge board, closes the sibling lane and device sync, and moves the PersonaGroup's keys (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges">>). The certificate withdrawal denies that vessel's face-scoped key at one Nexus's gates, and rides the face's own presentations. One removal signs the dyad raise once, and one withdrawal for each face key the vessel held a certificate under. The root-holding vessel signs both, each with one signature.
281:* ''Neither act rides a Nexus board.'' A gate reads the withdrawal and never the dyad; a sibling reads both.
287:''A key's bytes never say whether it served before.'' So the law rests with the two hands that sign. A joining vessel names every key it holds for anything else (its roots, its other faces' keys) and its request refuses over any of them before its key signs. The root holder names its own other face keys, and every vessel key it certified under another face, and its face key signs no certificate over any of them. The three keys of one chain read pairwise distinct at every reader.
293:''A session opens before its certificate'' (operator ruling, approved as presented). The vessel's own chain verifies first: the face key, its certificate, this vessel's face-scoped key, with no learned denial. The face supplier joins THAT to the admit and the private grant, and the owner opens the session wearing the face. Only then does the signer mint the session's key and its certificate under the vessel's face-scoped key. The owner answers no caller between the two acts: one door opens the session and mints its signer, and a mint that fails closes the session it opened before any message routes to it. No session ever stands live with a face and no signer.
297:''A session's face names one PersonaGroup at ONE Nexus'' (house reading). The proof subject and the admit both stand per Nexus, and the session contract's face carries one of each. A face that works at two Nexuses holds one signer for each (#/the-key-hierarchy); whether one session carries both or two sessions carry one each stays the session contract's word to give.
306:* one Keyhive instance wears one identifier inside every group it joins, so two faces of one vessel would share it.
316:''The sibling-facing proof moves to face scope together with the herm-facing stamp.'' The channel seals a face-scoped session proof and the face-private membership authority to its sibling, and binds its Repo peer id to the proven face session. It transmits no device delegation and derives no peer id from a device key. The herm stamps the separate per-dial relay identity alone.
326:!! An offline root holder ~ nobody new joins
328:''A new vessel joins by the root holder's act.'' That vessel holds the face key, and the face key certifies a joining vessel's own key. So while the root-holding vessel stays offline:
330:* a joined vessel keeps using the certificate it holds, under the face key the Nexus already admitted;
331:* ''nobody new joins'': no certificate issues, and a wiped device cannot return;
343:''What stands in code'': the leaf identity loads the vessel's seed and ContactCard (`packages/lararium-node/src/leaf-identity.ts`); the auth wire binds a presentation to the vessel key (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/auth-wire.ts`); the node gate sends `lar:challenge` before any proof (`packages/lararium-node/src/daemon-auth-gate.ts`); the PersonaGroup ring looks up grants by vessel key, and its own-hand path skips the check (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-group-ring.ts`); the sibling proof sends a device edge inside its sealed box (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/leaf-peer-proof.ts`).
348:* ''Private authority'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-authority.ts`): minting, the request, the certificate, the withdrawal, the fold of a reader's memory, and the chain check with its six verdicts. Its callers: the fleet-join door, the removal door and the gate. It also reads a vessel's OWN chain with no session key in it (`checkVesselChain`), which a session opens on.
350:* ''The gate bundle'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-bundle.ts`): the floor and sovereign tiers with their typed routes and roots (the serving tier stands owed with the ember's train E), the pin, its digest, the knock, the prologue, and the transport and signing keys one gate derives per (tier, Nexus, route). Nothing rests. Its caller: the vessel's gate composer.
352:* ''The gate'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-gate.ts`): the whole exchange against a socket and the collaborators it asks: a deadline source, an admit reader over the one presenter-verifier, the shadow ring, the grant ring, the sorter, a withdrawal memory and a session table. Every silence names ONE reason from the gate's fixed vocabulary on the vessel's own log (`GATE_SILENCE_REASONS`), with at most one detail drawn from a vocabulary the gate already types; no thrown error's text and no string a collaborator spelled reaches that log. A first flight of any length but 96 bytes draws silence before any Noise work. Its caller: the socket cutover.
355:* ''The session face supplier'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/session-face.ts`): it joins a verified chain, this vessel's own key for the face, the admit's verdict and the private grant into the face a session wears, and verifies none of the last three itself (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/what-a-session-carries">>). Its callers: the same two.
359:* ''Two custody rows.'' The face proof key rests cold with the root holder, one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus); a vessel's face-scoped key rests hot on its own vessel, one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus). Each holds 32 secret bytes and reads whole or refuses by name. The modules name the rows and the two signing doors they ask of the custody seat, and write no row.
361:* ''The private grant's reader.'' The record that binds a face key to its PersonaGroup at one Nexus rests in the group's private causal state, and the ring reads it by vessel key today.
363:* ''The floor and sovereign roots' doors'' stand at their seats (the serving key's door stands owed with train E), and no composer calls them until the socket cutover. A floor bundle asks the floor key's derive door (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`), which answers while the vessel rests locked, hands back a fresh child its caller overwrites and never the floor key. A sovereign bundle asks the custody seat's (`custodyGateDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-seat.ts`), which answers only while the seat holds its key. The two doors carry two types, so a line that hands one tier's door to the other fails to compile; a root refuses a door of the other tier and a door no mint made by name (`door-other-tier`); and each seat's door refuses the other tier's child by name. A door states only what its minter claimed, so one weld holds the minters to the two seats: a second caller of either mint, anywhere in source, turns it red (`packages/lararium-node/tests/gate-root-door-minters.test.ts`). The composer owes the call.
365:* ''A held Nexus root per gate'': the roster, the charter lineage, the deny board and the antigen that the admit reader folds, with the causal position it read them at. A gate that holds no root refuses every admit by name.
366:* ''The rings by face key.'' The shadow ring owes a reading over one presenter (set aside at this gate; set aside from this vessel's own fleet), and the grant ring owes the private record by face key. Both read by vessel key today.
367:* ''The sorter over proven facts'': a class from a held admit, a private grant, the fleet reading and the arm as presented, over ONE Nexus's posture.
373:* ''The withdrawal proof under a live partition.'' The fold and the six verdicts stand proven as pure functions over synthetic histories, and the six answers stand proven at one gate against a dialer in the same process, partitioned by hand. No two gates and no real herm have yet carried one.
380:# ''The gate, behind interfaces.'' Specified in code, with the bundle's root, the held Nexus root, the rings by face key and the sorter over proven facts owed.
382:# ''Sibling carriage.'' The herm dial, the inner proof and the sibling peer id move to face scope.
